{"hash":"a2ca1ff78c80f928","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":77.906,"said":"Look at these stones beside the great temple, set in steps but holding up nothing. This is the foundation of an older shrine, and historians link it with the twelfth century, when the Chaulukya king Kumarapala had Somnath rebuilt in fine stone. His minister Bhava Brihaspati, who oversaw that work, is said to have raised temples here to Parvati, to Bhimeshvara and to Siddheshvara. What remains of the Parvati shrine is mostly this base: its walls went the way of so much at Somnath, through the troubled centuries that struck the temple again and again. Even modern writers describe it with a little wonder, as a mysterious stone foundation. The Mother has not been forgotten, though. Inside the sanctum Maa Parvati stands right behind the Jyotirlinga, and in 2021 the foundation stone was laid for a new Shree Parvati Temple at Somnath, in the traditional Sompura style of Gujarat’s temple builders. Here, then, the old and the new meet: a plinth from a vanished age, and a shrine rising again for the Goddess.\n\nDid you know? The Parvati shrine was one of several temples traditionally credited to Bhava Brihaspati, who oversaw Kumarapala’s rebuilding of Somnath.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1013},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1029,"end":1166}],"words":[[0.098,0.228,0,4],[0.338,0.12,5,7],[0.471,0.228,8,13],[0.711,0.469,14,20],[1.192,0.469,21,27],[1.673,0.108,28,31],[1.793,0.337,32,37],[2.141,0.505,38,44],[2.85,0.264,46,49],[3.127,0.108,50,52],[3.247,0.433,53,58],[3.692,0.204,59,62],[3.908,0.385,63,70],[4.305,0.168,71,73],[4.485,0.553,74,81],[5.913,0.264,83,87],[6.19,0.108,88,90],[6.31,0.084,91,94],[6.406,0.565,95,105],[6.983,0.132,106,108],[7.127,0.108,109,111],[7.248,0.288,112,117],[7.548,0.469,118,124],[8.269,0.156,126,129],[8.438,0.649,130,140],[9.099,0.264,141,145],[9.375,0.132,146,148],[9.519,0.108,149,153],[9.639,0.096,154,157],[9.748,0.445,158,165],[10.204,0.625